# T-1066 expansion stack: mutually recursive type aliases.
# EvenList and OddList reference each other — neither is self-referential alone.
# The expansion stack cycle detection (expand_named Steps 4-5) handles this:
#   1. Push (arc_EvenList, fresh_var_even) to stack; begin expanding EvenList body
#   2. Body references OddList — push (arc_OddList, fresh_var_odd); expand OddList body
#   3. OddList body references EvenList — Arc::ptr_eq finds it in stack → RecursiveRef(var_even)
#   4. Pop OddList: body has no var_odd → no Recursive wrap (OddList is NOT the cycle origin)
#   5. Pop EvenList: body contains var_even → wrap in Recursive (EvenList IS the cycle origin)
#
# This test verifies that the expansion stack does not diverge (infinite loop) on
# mutually recursive aliases — the correct structural representation terminates.
#
# At runtime: both are structural [or ...] aliases — TypeAlias entries are skipped.
# No constructor injection (the or-form bodies are not named constructors).
# Only `result` appears in the dict output.
#
# At typecheck: no warnings — the recursive alias is well-formed (record body is guarded).
[
  EvenList: [type [or Absent [head: Int  tail: OddList]]]
  OddList:  [type [or Absent [head: Int  tail: EvenList]]]
  result: true
]
=== out
Dict({"EvenList": Dict({}), "OddList": Dict({}), "result": Bool(true)})
